<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariconium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariconium was a Romano-British town near Weston-under-Penyard, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ross-on-wye">Ross-on-Wye</a> in Herefordshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">iron-working</a> that used ore from the Forest of Dean, and it gave its name to the post-Roman Welsh kingdom of Ergyng, later the English district of Archenfield.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariconium.jpg" alt="Ariconium" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pauline Eccles · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_over_Bury_Hill_-_geograph.org.uk_-_906849.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ariconium lay at Bury Hill in the parish of Weston under Penyard, about 5 km east of Ross on Wye and about 24 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>. The Forest of Dean and its surroundings were an ancient source of iron ore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>, and the hillside, where the lie of the land increases the flow of air, suited bloomeries, furnaces that produced imperfect iron together with cinders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> added bellows, which gave a hotter blast and better iron that needed further refining. The cinder refuse was dumped in great piles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was occupied by Britons before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, probably already for iron working, and continuously through the Roman period, when its industry grew. There was a focus of settlement near Bromsash. Its only mention in classical sources is as a station on Iter XIII of the Iter Britanniarum of the Antonine Itineraries. Finds include pre-Roman British coins, one minted by Cunobelin, and Roman coins down to 360 and none later. It seems to have been abandoned soon after 360, perhaps violently, which may be linked to the collapse of authority and widespread raiding that Ammianus reports; no later occupation has been found.</p><p>William Camden placed Ariconium at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnis">Kenchester</a>, but later study of the itineraries, notably by John Horsley, fixed it at Bury Hill. Local people clearing the overgrown site uncovered huge cinder piles and the walls of buildings; the refuse covers about 40 hectares. The name's origin is uncertain. It is generally believed to be the source of the name of the post-Roman kingdom of Ergyng, though the site lay outside that kingdom's later bounds, and both may derive from an older name for a wider area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>What is known of the site comes from research and excavation, which show iron working throughout its existence. Since 2008 the name has been used for a united benefice of six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> parishes in the area: Aston Ingham, Hope Mansell, Lea, Linton, Upton Bishop and Weston under Penyard.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ariconium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ergyng emerged after Roman rule ended, its name derived from <b>Ariconium</b>, an iron-working town near Ross-on-Wye.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ergyng">Kingdom of Ergyng</a></li></ul></section>
